Native` Promise.all` always returns results in the order you passed the promises in, so when values end up in the wrong variable, the cause is destructuring that no longer matches that order.


This kind of bug survives code review. A loader gets one more request added, the tests that cover it still pass, and a page shows a company name where the user’s theme should be.


This article shows the refactor that causes it, explains why the native method is not at fault, and gives you typed keyed helpers you can use today. It also covers the TC39 proposal that adds keyed versions of these methods to the language.


## Key Takeaways


- ` Promise.all` never reorders its results: the fulfilled array follows the order of the input promises, not the order in which they settled.
- When a refactor adds or moves a promise without updating the array destructuring, values shift into the wrong variables and nothing throws.
- TypeScript catches a misaligned destructuring only where a shifted value is used, and only if the swapped values have different shapes.
- If you pass an object of promises instead of an array, each result stays tied to its name, so reordering requests cannot misalign them.
- ` Promise.allKeyed` and` Promise.allSettledKeyed` are at Stage 3 in TC39. Firefox 155 ships them natively, and other engines need a polyfill from core-js or es-shims.


## The Refactor Bug: Values Shift With No Error


Positional destructuring breaks silently when someone inserts a promise in the middle of the array. The code below runs as an ES module because it uses top-level` await` :


```text
type   User   =   {   id  :   string  ;   name  :   string   };
type   Company   =   {   id  :   string  ;   name  :   string   };
type   Settings   =   {   theme  :   "  light  "   |   "  dark  "   };


const   getUser   =   async   (  id  :   string  )  :   Promise  <  User  >   =>   ({   id  ,   name  :   "  Ada  "   });
const   getCompany   =   async   (  id  :   string  )  :   Promise  <  Company  >   =>   ({   id  ,   name  :   "  Acme  "   });
const   getSettings   =   async   (  _id  :   string  )  :   Promise  <  Settings  >   =>   ({   theme  :   "  dark  "   });


// Before: correct
const   [  user  ,   company  ]   =   await   Promise  .  all  ([  getUser  (  "  1  "  ),   getCompany  (  "  1  "  )]);


// After a refactor: getSettings inserted in the middle, the new name appended at the end
const   [  user2  ,   company2  ,   settings2  ]   =   await   Promise  .  all  ([
getUser  (  "  1  "  ),
getSettings  (  "  1  "  ),
getCompany  (  "  1  "  ),
]);


console  .  log  (  company2  .  name  );   // TS error: Property 'name' does not exist on type 'Settings'
```


` company2` now holds the settings object and` settings2` holds the company. TypeScript does not report an error on the destructuring line. It infers each variable’s type from its position in the tuple, so` company2` simply becomes` Settings` . You only get an error where the code reads` company2.name` .


The same mistake can also compile without any error:


```text
// User and Company share a shape, so swapping them type-checks
const   [  company3  ,   user3  ]   =   await   Promise  .  all  ([  getUser  (  "  1  "  ),   getCompany  (  "  1  "  )]);
```


If two requests return the same type, or both return` any` (which is what an untyped` response.json()` gives you), a reordered` Promise.all` compiles cleanly and runs with the wrong data.


## Does Promise.all Preserve Order?


Yes.` Promise.all` preserves input order, so it is not at fault here. MDN states that the fulfilled array holds the values[in the order of the promises passed, regardless of completion order](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all) . Index 0 belongs to the first input and index 1 to the second, even if the second promise settles first.


The` Promise.all` order guarantee is purely positional. Nothing links a result to the name you destructure it into. If the array of promises and the destructuring pattern stop matching, the method still does its job and your variables get the wrong values.


Results do get scrambled in one other case: a hand-written` Promise.all` that calls` push()` on each result as it arrives. That version returns values in completion order. The native method avoids this by writing each result to the index of its input.


## Fix It Today With a Typed allKeyed Helper


The fix for mixed-up` Promise.all` results is to pass an object of promises instead of an array. Each result then comes back under its own name, so adding, removing or reordering a request cannot move a value into the wrong variable.


```text
type   Resolved<  T  >   =   {   [  K   in   keyof   T  ]  :   Awaited  <  T  [  K  ]>   };


export   async   function   allKeyed  <  T   extends   Record  <  string  ,   Promise  <  unknown  >>>(
obj  :   T  ,
)  :   Promise  <{ [  K   in   keyof   T  ]  :   Awaited  <  T  [  K  ]> }> {
const   entries   =   Object  .  entries  (  obj  );
const   values   =   await   Promise  .  all  (  entries  .  map  (([,   promise  ])   =>   promise  ));
return   Object  .  fromEntries  (
entries  .  map  (([  key  ],   i  )   =>   [  key  ,   values  [  i  ]]),
)   as   unknown   as   Resolved  <  T  >;
}


const   {   user  ,   company  ,   settings   }   =   await   allKeyed  ({
user  :   getUser  (  "  1  "  ),
settings  :   getSettings  (  "  1  "  ),
company  :   getCompany  (  "  1  "  ),
});
```


The keys and the promises both come from a single` Object.entries` call. The helper then matches each value back to its key by index in that same array, so the order in which the object lists its keys cannot cause a mismatch. The double assertion is there because` Object.fromEntries` returns a string-indexed record, and TypeScript cannot work out the per-key types from it. Each destructured variable is still inferred correctly (` User` ,` Company` ,` Settings` ).


Two constraints apply.[Object.fromEntries](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/fromEntries) requires` es2019` or later in your tsconfig` lib` .[Object.entries](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/entries) skips symbol-keyed properties, so this helper ignores them.


## How Do You Get Keyed Results From Promise.allSettled?


To get every outcome instead of failing fast, use the same pattern with[Promise.allSettled](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/allSettled) . Each key then maps to a` PromiseSettledResult` :


```text
export   async   function   allSettledKeyed  <  T   extends   Record  <  string  ,   Promise  <  unknown  >>>(
obj  :   T  ,
)  :   Promise  <{ [  K   in   keyof   T  ]  :   PromiseSettledResult  <  Awaited  <  T  [  K  ]>> }> {
const   entries   =   Object  .  entries  (  obj  );
const   results   =   await   Promise  .  allSettled  (  entries  .  map  (([,   promise  ])   =>   promise  ));
return   Object  .  fromEntries  (
entries  .  map  (([  key  ],   i  )   =>   [  key  ,   results  [  i  ]]),
)   as   unknown   as   { [  K   in   keyof   T  ]  :   PromiseSettledResult  <  Awaited  <  T  [  K  ]>> };
}


const   results   =   await   allSettledKeyed  ({   user  :   getUser  (  "  1  "  ),   company  :   getCompany  (  "  1  "  )   });


if (  results  .  company  .  status   ===   "  fulfilled  "  ) {
console  .  log  (  results  .  company  .  value  .  name  );
} else {
console  .  error  (  results  .  company  .  reason  );
}
```


Checking` status` narrows the result, so` value` is available on fulfilled results and` reason` on rejected ones. This helper needs` es2020` in` lib` .


## What Are Promise.allKeyed and Promise.allSettledKeyed?


The[TC39 Await Dictionary proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-await-dictionary) adds` Promise.allKeyed` and` Promise.allSettledKeyed` to the language. It is at Stage 3, which means the design is close to final and engines can implement it.[Firefox 155 added both methods](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Mozilla/Firefox/Releases/155) . Other browsers and runtimes still need a polyfill.


With core-js, version 3.50.0 or later provides the polyfill, and the changelog lists a fix for sync promises in 3.51.0.[The core-js Await Dictionary docs](https://core-js.io/v3/docs/features/proposals/await-dictionary) list the entry points:


```text
import   "  core-js/actual/promise/all-keyed  "  ;
// or load both methods: import "core-js/proposals/await-dictionary";


const   {   user  ,   settings  ,   company   }   =   await   Promise  .  allKeyed  ({
user  :   getUser  (  "  1  "  ),
settings  :   getSettings  (  "  1  "  ),
company  :   getCompany  (  "  1  "  ),
});
```


The es-shims alternative is[promise.allkeyed](https://github.com/es-shims/Promise.allKeyed) . Install it with` npm i promise.allkeyed` , then call` require('promise.allkeyed/shim')()` .


The proposal differs from the userland helper in two ways. It uses all own enumerable keys, including symbols, while` Object.entries` helpers skip symbols. It also fulfills with a null-prototype object, while` Object.fromEntries` returns an ordinary object.


