Userland` allKeyed` Key Yes No Everywhere (lib es2019+)


` Promise.allKeyed` Key Yes Yes Firefox 155+; polyfill elsewhere


## Rejections Still Fail Fast


The` allKeyed` and` allSettledKeyed` helpers above are thin wrappers, so they behave exactly like the methods they call.` Promise.all` rejects with the first rejection reason it sees, ignores any later rejections, and does not cancel the operations that are still running. Later rejections do not fire` unhandledrejection` , because` Promise.all` attaches handlers to every input.[Promise.allKeyed also rejects](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/allKeyed) with the first rejection reason.


If a fetcher can throw synchronously before it returns a promise, wrap it in[Promise.try](https://blog.openreplay.com/clean-async-chains-promise-try/) so the throw becomes a rejection. If the whole call has no handler, you end up with an[Uncaught (in promise) TypeError](https://blog.openreplay.com/fix-uncaught-in-promise-typeerror/) .


## Conclusion


` Promise.all` returns results in input order. The mismatch is between your array of promises and your destructuring pattern. Replace positional destructuring in your data loaders with the typed` allKeyed` helper now, and switch to` Promise.allKeyed` once your target engines ship it or your polyfill covers them. To see how other languages handle concurrent awaits, read OpenReplay’s comparison of[async/await in JavaScript vs Rust vs Python](https://blog.openreplay.com/async-await-javascript-rust-python/) .


## FAQs


Does Promise.all keep the order of results when used with Array.map?


Yes. Promise.all(items.map(fetchItem)) returns results in the same order as items, because each result is stored at the index of its input promise. It does not matter which request finishes first. Non-promise values in the array are passed through and keep their position too. Order only breaks if a custom helper pushes results as they arrive.


Does Promise.allKeyed return a plain object?


No. Promise.allKeyed fulfills with a null-prototype object that has the same keys as the input, in the same order. A null-prototype object inherits nothing from Object.prototype, so methods like hasOwnProperty and toString are missing. Use Object.hasOwn or the in operator instead. A userland helper built on Object.fromEntries returns an ordinary object with the usual prototype.


Does Promise.allKeyed await promises inside nested objects?


No. Promise.allKeyed only processes the own enumerable properties of the object you pass in. A nested object counts as a single value and comes back as-is, so any promises inside it are still pending in the result. To await nested groups, call Promise.allKeyed on each inner object and pass the promises it returns as properties of the outer object.


How do I get keyed results while limiting concurrency?


Neither Promise.all nor Promise.allKeyed can limit concurrency. They only wait for promises that have already started. The p-map library has pMapKeyed and pMapSettledKeyed, which take an object or a Map plus a mapper function and a concurrency option. Like Promise.allKeyed, they return a null-prototype object with the same keys and include symbol keys. They also work in engines that don't ship the proposal yet.


